<h2>Graphics Computer</h2>

<p>
To start the <strong>Graphics Computer</strong> (a DIY ROM cartridge to convert the RCA Studio II to a graphics computer), select the CART file <tt>computer.st2</tt> and start the Studio II.
</p>

<p>
After start-up, four modes may be selected using keys <tt>1</tt>-<tt>4</tt> (see sections below).
</p>

<p>
When you have completed your entries in one mode and wish to switch to another mode, press Numpad <tt>0</tt>. <strong>DO NOT PRESS RESET.</strong> Pressing reset will destroy part of the program you have just entered.
</p>

<p>
After Numpad <tt>0</tt> has been released, you may select the next mode. Pressing Numpad <tt>0</tt> also causes the control program to store most of the CDP1802 internal 16-bit registers in memory so they can be examined using the memory read mode. This is very useful when trying to debug a program.
</p>

<p>
All registers except R0, R1, R2 and R8 are stored in RAM at addresses <tt>09B3</tt> to <tt>09CF</tt>. The last digit of the address is the register number; if the third address digit is <tt>B</tt>, it is the lower-order register address. For example, <tt>09B5</tt> contains the contents of CDP1802 register R5.0, while <tt>09C5</tt> contains the contents of register R5.1.
</p>

<h4>Load Mode (key <tt>1</tt>)</h4>

<p>
When key <tt>1</tt> is pressed, the Studio II waits for the starting address of the program you intend to load. You enter this address in hexadecimal notation using keys <tt>0</tt>-<tt>9</tt> for digits 0 to 9 and Numpad <tt>1</tt>-<tt>6</tt> for A-F (A=Numpad <tt>1</tt>, B=Numpad <tt>2</tt>, C=Numpad <tt>3</tt>, D=Numpad <tt>4</tt>, E=Numpad <tt>5</tt>, F=Numpad <tt>6</tt>). The address will be displayed on the screen.
</p>

<p>
The normal start address is <tt>0800</tt>, and your program can normally extend from there to address <tt>08FB</tt>. There is also RAM from address <tt>0900</tt> to <tt>09FF</tt>; this RAM is normally bit-mapped onto the display, but may be used for program storage if your program does not need a full display.
</p>

<p>
After you enter the four-digit start address, you next enter the first byte of the program. Then continue to key in your code one byte after another until you are done. The address display automatically increments as you enter each byte, and each data byte is displayed as it is entered.
</p>

<h4>Memory-Read Mode (key <tt>2</tt>)</h4>

<p>
When key <tt>2</tt> is pressed, enter the address of the memory location you wish to examine. Pressing any key (<tt>0</tt>-<tt>9</tt>) will then display the byte stored at that location.
</p>

<p>
Continuing to press any key (<tt>0</tt>-<tt>9</tt>) will sequentially step through memory, displaying its contents and incrementing the address display at the same time.
</p>

<h4>Run Mode (key <tt>3</tt>)</h4>

<p>
The program that has just been loaded can be run by pressing key <tt>3</tt> and then entering the four-digit start address of the program. The program will start running as soon as the last digit of the start address has been entered and will use R9 as the program counter.
</p>

<h4>Shift Mode (key <tt>4</tt>)</h4>

<p>
Suppose you have loaded a program and then want to add several bytes in the middle of it. Press key <tt>4</tt> and then enter a four-digit start address to shift the entire program, beginning with this address, one location higher in memory.
</p>

<p>
The shifting is done by pressing either key <tt>8</tt> or key <tt>9</tt>. Press key <tt>8</tt> to shift page 8 (hex <tt>800</tt>-<tt>8FB</tt>) or press key <tt>9</tt> to shift page 9 (hex <tt>900</tt>-<tt>9FF</tt>). Each key press shifts the entire program after the start address one location higher in memory.
</p>


<h2>Hockey</h2>

<p>
This is an improved tennis game designed by Paul Robson for the RCA Studio II.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
Player A
--------
Up Arrow      up
Down Arrow    down
SPACE         serve

Player B
--------
Numpad 8 (W)      up
Numpad 2 (Z)      down
Numpad 0 (TAB)    serve
</pre>

<p><strong>Start</strong></p>

<pre>
1     Hockey
2     Soccer (or doubles tennis)
3     Pong
4     Squash
</pre>

<p>
Then select the difficulty using:
</p>

<pre>
8     Easy
9     Hard
</pre>


<h2>Kaboom</h2>

<p>
Freeware game developed by Paul Robson based on the Atari 2600 classic.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
Left Arrow     left
Right Arrow    right
SPACE          start
</pre>


<h2>Outbreak</h2>

<p>
Developed by Lee Romanow.
</p>

<p>
Press one of the following keys:
</p>

<pre>
0     Novice
1     Standard
2     Advanced
3     Expert
</pre>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
Up Arrow      move up
Left Arrow    move left
Right Arrow   move right
Down Arrow    move down
</pre>

<p>
Press both directions simultaneously for double speed.
Once the game is over, press <tt>SPACE</tt> to start a new game.
</p>


<h2>Pacman</h2>

<p>
Freeware game developed by Paul Robson.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
Up Arrow       up
Left Arrow     left
Right Arrow    right
Down Arrow     down
SPACE          start
</pre>

<p>
The controls are directional, i.e., they are used to change direction rather than to maintain movement.
</p>

<p>
It includes most of the standard features of Pacman but omits more advanced features due to memory, video resolution, or processor limitations. There are no bonus lives for reaching 10,000 points (or whatever). If you lose a life, you have to start again with the whole maze filled in.
</p>


<h2>Rocket</h2>

<p>
Conversion of the game written by Joseph Weisbecker, published in Byte magazine (1978).
</p>

<p>
Press <tt>1</tt> or <tt>TAB</tt> to start.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
SPACE     fire
</pre>


<p>
After 9 rockets, press reset to start a new game.
</p>


<h2>Scramble</h2>

<p>
Freeware game developed by Paul Robson.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
TAB / 6 / Numpad 6    start
Up Arrow              up
Down Arrow            down
Left Arrow            brake
SPACE                 fire
</pre>

<p>
At the start it displays G?. Press <tt>2</tt> for easy, <tt>4</tt> for medium, <tt>6</tt> for hard.
</p>

<p>
You will need to refuel periodically by shooting or bombing fuel dumps.
</p>


<h2>Space Invaders</h2>

<p>
This is a <strong>Space Invaders</strong> game designed by Paul Robson for the RCA Studio II.
</p>

<p>
There are 4 rows of 8 invaders and no mother ship due to screen space and memory limitations.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>

<pre>
Left Arrow     left
Right Arrow    right
SPACE          fire
TAB            restart
</pre>


<h2>TV Arcade 2012</h2>

<p>
Developed by Lee Romanow.
</p>

<h4>Craps</h4>

<p>
Press <tt>1</tt> to start.
</p>

<p><strong>Gameplay</strong></p>

<p>
A pair of numbers are shown on the screen. The number on the left represents the sum of two dice, the number on the right is money in the player's wallet.
</p>

<p>
To place your bet, press a non-zero key (<tt>1</tt>-<tt>9</tt>) which corresponds to a bet from $1.00 to $9.00. The dice will automatically be thrown.
</p>

<p>
If the total on the first roll is 7 or 11, you win the amount of your bet. If the total is 2, 3 or 12, you lose your bet.
</p>

<p>
If you roll 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 or 10, the round continues. Re-roll the dice by pressing any non-zero key until you roll the same number (win twice your bet), or lose by rolling 7.
</p>

<p><strong>End of round</strong></p>

<p>
The end of the round is indicated by a long series of beeps, and the player's wallet is updated.
</p>

<p>
If the balance is above $0.00, the game continues. If the balance is $0.00, the game is over. Press <tt>SPACE</tt> or Numpad <tt>1</tt> to start a new game.
</p>


<h4>Moon Lander</h4>

<p>
Press <tt>2</tt> to start.
</p>

<p>
Distance to the surface, downward velocity, and available fuel are shown as three numbers on the screen:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Distance (<tt>x</tt>) on the left</li>
<li>Velocity (<tt>v</tt>) in the centre</li>
<li>Fuel (<tt>f</tt>) on the right</li>
</ul>

<p>
Each turn you burn from 0 to 9 units of fuel by pressing that number (<tt>0</tt>-<tt>9</tt>). If you request more fuel than is available, the computer will burn what remains.
</p>

<p>
The computer calculates new values for <tt>x</tt>, <tt>v</tt>, and <tt>f</tt>, and updates the display.
</p>

<p>
While <tt>x</tt> is greater than 0, the game continues.
</p>

<p>
When <tt>x</tt> reaches 0, the lander has reached the surface and the game ends. You win if the final value of <tt>v</tt> is not higher than 5.
</p>

<p>
Press <tt>SPACE</tt> or Numpad <tt>1</tt> to start a new game.
</p>

<h4>Repeat After Me</h4>

<p>
Press <tt>3</tt> to start.
</p>

<p>
The game begins with the computer playing a random sequence. Numbers appear briefly near the centre of the screen, each accompanied by a beep sound.
</p>

<p>
Try to memorise the order of the numbers as they are played. When the sequence is finished, the computer waits for your input.
</p>

<p>
Replay the sequence using keys <tt>1</tt>, <tt>2</tt>, <tt>3</tt>, and <tt>4</tt>. The computer checks each entry as you press it.
</p>

<p>
If the sequence is entered correctly, one additional number is added and the game continues.
</p>

<p>
If you make a mistake, the game ends with a long series of beeps and your score is shown on the screen.
</p>

<p>
If you successfully memorise a sequence of 25 numbers, the game ends with a perfect score.
</p>

<p>
Press <tt>SPACE</tt> or Numpad <tt>1</tt> to start a new game.
</p>

<h4>Space Rescue</h4>

<p>
Press <tt>4</tt> to start.
</p>

<p>
Randomly placed asteroids scroll down from the top of the screen.
</p>

<p>
Avoid hitting the asteroids for as long as possible.
</p>

<p>
Steer the spaceship using the <tt>Left Arrow</tt> and <tt>Right Arrow</tt> keys. Movement is limited to within the screen edges.
</p>

<p>
If the ship collides with an asteroid, a long series of beeps will be heard and the game ends.
</p>

<p>
Press <tt>SPACE</tt> or Numpad <tt>1</tt> to start a new game.
</p>

<h4>Nim</h4>

<p>
Press one of the following keys:
</p>

<pre>
5     Nim 1 (last stone loses)
6     Nim 2 (last stone wins)
</pre>

<p>
The computer displays three rows of numbers on the screen:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Top row: number of games won (player 1 on the left, player 2 on the right)</li>
<li>Middle row: number of stones remaining in the pile</li>
<li>Bottom row: indicates whose turn it is</li>
</ul>

<p>
Each player's keyboard is active only during their turn.
</p>

<p>
Player 1 starts when the game begins.
</p>

<p>
On each turn, remove 1 to 3 stones by pressing <tt>1</tt>, <tt>2</tt>, or <tt>3</tt> on the active keyboard.
</p>

<p>
After a move, the pile is updated and the other player's turn begins.
</p>

<p>
Press <tt>0</tt> to reset the game, including scores.
</p>

<p>
Players alternate turns until the last stone is taken, ending the game.
</p>

<p>
The winner scores a point. A new game then starts, with the previous loser taking the first turn.
</p>
</body>
</html>
